And the high spirit which he carried into his
undertakings rendered his ambition finally invincible, so that it subdued not only enemies, but
even times and places;

The Getae did not sustain even the first charge of the cavalry;
for Alexander's bold stroke came as a great shock to them, in that he so easily crossed the Ister,
the greatest of rivers, in one night without so much as bridging the stream;

The
Thebans did not learn of his passage of the Gates until his arrival with all his force at
Onchestus;

They (the Uxians), however, astounded at Alexander's swiftness, and
over mastered at the very position in which they had chiefly put their trust, fled without so
much as coming to close quarters;

Satibarzanes, for his part, learning of
Alexander's proximity and astounded at the swiftness of his approach, fled with a few Areian
horsemen;

And all that had to be done in uncertainty he did with the utmost daring;
he was the most skilled in swift anticipation and gripping of his enemy before anyone had time
